INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti ransomware leaks and public attribution sources", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002; SRC-003; SRC-004", "text": "Benchmark source SRC-001 (Rewards for Justice) says Conti, also known as Wizard Spider, is assessed by the U.S. government as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says Conti was first detected in 2019, used in more than 1,000 operations, and victimized more than 400 organizations worldwide, including more than 290 in the U.S.; it describes theft plus encryption and publication/sale of stolen data if ransom is unpaid. It says online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti, with associated location Russia, and lists associated applications Conti RaaS, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. SRC-002 (Rapid7) reports that after Conti's pro-Russia statement following Russia's invasion of Ukraine, @ContiLeaks released 14 MB of internal chats in 393 JSON files on Feb. 27, 2022, with some encrypted/incomplete; leaks included internal communications, tool screenshots, exploit/design discussions, credentials and alleged infrastructure details, plus alleged source code and an alleged developer identity. Rapid7 assesses the scale suggested a senior member or coalition of disgruntled affiliates. Chats portrayed recruitment, coding exercises, salary negotiations, product discussions, software development, phishing, command/control and compromised-host management; Rapid7 assessed Conti as organized, funded, and still active in March 2022. SRC-003 (Center for Internet Security) describes Conti as a notorious double-extortion RaaS, with apparent ties to TrickBot. It says Conti leaks exposed a corporate-style operation: coders, testers, administrators, reverse engineers, penetration testers/hackers, and externally contracted spamming; separate budgets, borrowing between units, and an insider claim of more than 60 employees in July 2021. It reports spending on Cobalt Strike, security/antivirus testing, and Windows 11 reverse engineering. It explains RaaS as malware leased to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, and says IABs can sell established access. It reports Conti-related SLTT attacks and an open-source reported tie to Ryuk, but these are reporting/assessment rather than adjudicated findings. SRC-004 (DOJ Middle District of Tennessee) identifies case 3:23-CR-00092 defendants Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, and Andrey Zhuykov, charged in Sept. 2023 with conspiracy to violate CFAA and wire fraud for roles in the Conti ransomware conspiracy; and case 3:23-CR-00088 defendant Oleksii Lytvynenko, charged in Nov. 2025 with conspiracy to deploy Conti, and recorded as pleading guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud on June 10, 2026. DOJ explicitly states defendants are presumed innocent unless proven guilty, while the Lytvynenko page reports the plea and future sentencing date. DOJ says Conti affected more than 900 businesses, nonprofits, governments, and other victims."}
